#: conf.json:summary
msgid "Generic quaternions for MoonBit with arithmetic, normalization, 3D rotation, interpolation and Euler-angle conversion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:1
msgid "core API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:3
msgid "This page lists every public item of the package `Luna-Flow/quaternion` (source root `src`), grouped by purpose. The [generated interface](../../../src/pkg.generated.mbti) is the authority for signatures. The examples import the package under its default alias `@quaternion` and luna-generic as `@lf_alg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:13
msgid "The mathematics behind the operations is derived in the [core design](../design/core.md); the [core tutorial](../tutorial/core.md) walks through typical tasks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:15
msgid "The quaternion type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:19
msgid "`Quaternion[T]` is the quaternion $w + xi + yj + zk$ with components of type `T`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:25
msgid "A value stores a scalar (real) part $w$ and a vector part $(x, y, z)$. The type is abstract: build values with `Quaternion::from_vec`, `id`, `Quaternion::zero`, `Quaternion::one`, `from_axis_angle` or `from_euler`, and read them through `Quaternion::dot`, `Quaternion::map`, `to_string` or `Debug`. There are no component accessors (see [reading components](../tutorial/core.md#read-the-components-back))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:27
msgid "`T` is not constrained by the type. Each function asks only for the traits it uses: addition needs `Add`, the Hamilton product needs `Mul + Sub + Add`, the luna-generic structure needs `@lf_alg.Ring`, and anything with square roots or trigonometry needs `DoubleConvert`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:29
msgid "The derived `Eq` and `Hash` compare components exactly. `Debug` shows the stored fields:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:39
msgid "[!NOTE] `q` and `-q` describe the same rotation, but they are different quaternions and compare unequal. Compare rotations with `Quaternion::dot` instead (see the [tutorial](../tutorial/core.md#compare-two-rotations))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:42
msgid "Construction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:46
msgid "`Quaternion::from_vec((w, x, y, z))` builds $w + xi + yj + zk$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:52
msgid "The first tuple element is the scalar part. No trait is required, so any component type works."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:63
msgid "`id()` returns the identity $1 + 0i + 0j + 0k$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:69
msgid "It equals `Quaternion::one()` but asks for `Num`, which every luna-generic numeric type (`Int`, `Int64`, `Double`, ...) implements. As a rotation it is the identity rotation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:73
msgid "`Quaternion::zero()` returns the additive identity $0 + 0i + 0j + 0k$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:81
msgid "`Quaternion::one()` returns the multiplicative identity $1 + 0i + 0j + 0k$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:87
msgid "`zero` and `one` are the luna-generic `Zero` and `One` methods promoted to static methods. They need fewer traits than `id`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:101
msgid "`from_axis_angle(axis, angle)` returns the unit quaternion of the rotation by `angle` radians around `axis`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:107
msgid "The result is $\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\,\\hat n$ with $\\hat n = \\text{axis}/|\\text{axis}|$. The axis does not have to be normalized, but it must be non-zero: a zero axis divides by zero and gives NaN components for `Double`. The rotation is counter-clockwise when you look down the axis toward the origin (right-hand rule). Sines, cosines and the axis length are computed in `Double`, so `T` needs `DoubleConvert`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:118
msgid "`from_euler(roll, pitch, yaw, order~)` builds a quaternion from three angles in radians."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:124
msgid "`order` defaults to `\"XYZ\"`. Write $q_X(\\alpha)$, $q_Y(\\alpha)$, $q_Z(\\alpha)$ for the rotations by $\\alpha$ around the coordinate axes. The accepted orders compute:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:126
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:126
msgid "Reading"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:128
msgid "`\"XYZ\"` (default)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:128
msgid "intrinsic X-Y-Z, equal to extrinsic Z-Y-X"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:129
msgid "intrinsic Y-X-Z"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:130
msgid "intrinsic Z-X-Y, angles taken by position"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:131
msgid "see the warning below"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:133
msgid "Any other string aborts with `order error. Please check input format and retry`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:145
msgid "[!WARNING] Known issues in the current implementation: `\"ZYX\"` and `\"YZX\"` return quaternions that are not unit length and do not correspond to any product of three axis rotations, so do not use them. `\"YXZ\"` assigns the angles by axis (roll about X, pitch about Y, yaw about Z), while `\"ZXY\"` assigns them by position (the first angle about Z). Prefer `\"XYZ\"`, or compose `from_axis_angle` rotations yourself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:148
msgid "`Quaternion::default` (deprecated method form)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:150
msgid "`Default::default()` returns `id()`; the method form is deprecated, see [Deprecated](#deprecated)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:152
msgid "Arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:156
msgid "`q + r` adds component-wise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:164
msgid "`q - r` subtracts component-wise; it is computed as `q + -r`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:172
msgid "`-q` negates every component."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:180
msgid "`q * r` is the Hamilton product."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:186
msgid "In scalar–vector form, $(s_1, \\mathbf v_1)(s_2, \\mathbf v_2) = (s_1 s_2 - \\mathbf v_1\\cdot\\mathbf v_2,\\ s_1\\mathbf v_2 + s_2\\mathbf v_1 + \\mathbf v_1\\times\\mathbf v_2)$. The product is associative and distributes over `+`, but it is **not commutative**: $qr - rq = 2\\,\\mathbf v_q \\times \\mathbf v_r$. For rotations, `q * r` applies `r` first and then `q`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:188
msgid "The four operators are also available as methods (`q.add(r)`, `q.mul(r)`, ...):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:205
msgid "`q / r` is right division: $q\\,r^{-1}$, the solution $x$ of $x\\,r = q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:211
msgid "It computes $q\\,\\bar r / |r|^2$ with one division per component and no intermediate inverse. Dividing by the zero quaternion divides by zero in `T`: NaN components for `Double`, a runtime trap for `Int`. Over `Int` every component is truncated by integer division, so the result is only meaningful when the divisions are exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:213
msgid "[!IMPORTANT] Since 0.2.0, `/` is right division. Before 0.2.0 it computed the left quotient $r^{-1} q$; use `Quaternion::left_div` for that."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:218
msgid "`q.left_div(r)` is left division: $r^{-1} q$, the solution $x$ of $r\\,x = q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:224
msgid "It computes $\\bar r\\,q / |r|^2$. The two quotients differ by $2\\,(\\mathbf v_q \\times \\mathbf v_r)/|r|^2$, so they agree exactly when the vector parts are parallel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:239
msgid "`q.scale(s)` multiplies every component by the scalar `s`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:245
msgid "Scalars are central in $\\mathbb H$, so `q.scale(s)` equals both `q * s` and `s * q` with `s` read as a real quaternion."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:249
msgid "`q.conjugate()` returns $\\bar q = w - xi - yj - zk$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:255
msgid "It is the luna-generic `Conjugate` method, promoted. Conjugation reverses products: $\\overline{qr} = \\bar r\\,\\bar q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:259
msgid "`q.inv()` returns the two-sided inverse $q^{-1} = \\bar q / |q|^2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:265
msgid "It is the luna-generic `Inverse` method, promoted; generic code calls it as `@lf_alg.Inverse::inv(q)`. For a unit quaternion the inverse is the conjugate. The zero quaternion has no inverse: over `Double` the result has NaN components. Over `Int` the factor `1 / |q|^2` is truncated, so `inv` returns zero unless $|q|^2 = 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:278
msgid "`q.pow_by_int(n)` returns $q^n$ for an `Int` exponent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:284
msgid "`n = 0` gives the identity and a negative `n` gives $(q^{-1})^{|n|}$. The power is computed by repeated squaring with $O(\\log |n|)$ multiplications. Powers of one quaternion commute with each other, so the order of the factors does not matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:288
msgid "`q.pow_by_T(t)` returns the real power $q^t$ through the polar form."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:294
msgid "Writing $q = |q|(\\cos\\varphi + \\hat n \\sin\\varphi)$, the result is $|q|^t(\\cos t\\varphi + \\hat n\\sin t\\varphi)$. The zero quaternion is returned unchanged. A real quaternion ($x = y = z = 0$) uses `@math.pow` on its scalar part, so a negative real part with a non-integer exponent gives NaN."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:305
msgid "[!WARNING] Known issue: `pow_by_T` recovers the angle as $\\varphi = \\arcsin|\\hat{\\mathbf v}|$, which lies in $[0, \\pi/2]$. It is wrong when the scalar part is negative ($\\varphi > \\pi/2$): the function then uses $\\pi - \\varphi$, so for example `q.pow_by_T(1.0)` returns `q` with the sign of its scalar part flipped. Use `pow_by_int` for integer exponents, and negate `q` first (which does not change the rotation) when you need fractional powers of a rotation with a negative scalar part."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:308
msgid "Norms and inner product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:312
msgid "`q.dot(r)` is the Euclidean inner product $w_q w_r + x_q x_r + y_q y_r + z_q z_r$ of the four components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:318
msgid "For unit quaternions, $|q\\cdot r| = \\cos(\\theta/2)$ where $\\theta$ is the angle of the rotation that takes one orientation to the other."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:322
msgid "`q.square_len()` returns $|q|^2 = w^2 + x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = q\\,\\bar q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:328
msgid "It needs only `Mul + Add`, so it is exact over `Int` and `BigInt`. The squared norm is multiplicative: $|qr|^2 = |q|^2|r|^2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:332
msgid "`q.magnitude()` returns $|q|$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:338
msgid "The square root is taken in `Double` and converted back with `DoubleConvert::from_double`, which truncates for `Int`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:342
msgid "`q.normalize()` returns $q / |q|$, the unit quaternion in the direction of `q`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:348
msgid "The zero quaternion is returned unchanged. The result has norm 1 up to rounding. Over `Int` the computation truncates and is not meaningful."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:361
msgid "Rotations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:365
msgid "`q.rotate(v)` rotates the 3D vector `v` by the unit quaternion `q`, that is it computes the vector part of $q\\,v\\,q^{-1}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:371
msgid "The implementation uses $\\mathbf t = 2\\,\\mathbf u \\times \\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf v' = \\mathbf v + w\\,\\mathbf t + \\mathbf u \\times \\mathbf t$ for $q = (w, \\mathbf u)$, which needs no division. That formula equals $q v q^{-1}$ only when $|q| = 1$; `rotate` does not normalize for you. The rotation follows the right-hand rule around the axis of `q`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:385
msgid "`slerp(q1, q2, t)` interpolates spherically between the rotations `q1` and `q2`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:391
msgid "Both inputs are normalized first. If their dot product is negative, `q2` is replaced by `-q2` (the same rotation) so the interpolation takes the shorter arc. With $\\cos\\Omega = q_1\\cdot q_2$ the result is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:397
msgid "When $\\cos\\Omega > 0.9995$ the function uses normalized linear interpolation instead, to avoid dividing by a tiny $\\sin\\Omega$. `t = 0` gives `q1` (normalized) and `t = 1` gives `q2` or `-q2`. Values of `t` outside $[0, 1]$ extrapolate along the same great circle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:411
msgid "`q.to_euler(order~, external~)` decomposes the rotation of `q` into three angles in radians."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:417
msgid "`q` is normalized first. `order` names three distinct axes and defaults to `\"ZYX\"`; `external` defaults to `true`. The k-th returned angle belongs to the k-th axis of `order`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:419
msgid "extrinsic (`external=true`), order $ABC$ with angles $(a, b, c)$: rotate about the fixed axis $A$ by $a$, then the fixed $B$ by $b$, then the fixed $C$ by $c$, so $q = \\pm\\,q_C(c)\\,q_B(b)\\,q_A(a)$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:420
msgid "intrinsic (`external=false`): rotate about $A$, then about the moved $B$, then the moved $C$, so $q = \\pm\\,q_A(a)\\,q_B(b)\\,q_C(c)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:422
msgid "The supported orders are `\"XYZ\"`, `\"XZY\"`, `\"YZX\"` and `\"ZYX\"`; any other string aborts with `order error. Please check input format and retry`. The middle angle lies in $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ and the outer angles in $(-\\pi, \\pi]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:424
msgid "When the sine of the middle angle reaches $|\\sin b| \\ge 0.9998$ (about $88.85^\\circ$), the decomposition is near gimbal lock: the function prints a `UserWarning: Gimbal lock detected...` line to standard output, sets the middle angle to exactly $\\pm\\pi/2$ and the third angle to $0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:436
msgid "[!WARNING] Known issues in the current implementation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:439
msgid "`order=\"XZY\"` with `external=true`, and `order=\"YZX\"` with `external=false`, do not compute the named sequence; they return the angles of intrinsic X-Y-Z (in the order X, Y, Z for the first and Z, Y, X for the second). The other six combinations are correct away from gimbal lock."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:440
msgid "In the gimbal-lock branch the returned angles do not reproduce the input rotation: the first angle is computed from a formula that does not isolate the remaining degree of freedom. Treat results with $|b| \\approx \\pi/2$ as unreliable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:441
msgid "Between $|\\sin b| = 0.9998$ and $1$ the middle angle is snapped to $\\pm\\pi/2$, an error of up to about $0.02$ rad."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:445
msgid "`q.to_euler_external_XYZ()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"XYZ\", external=true)`: angles $(a, b, c)$ about X, Y, Z with $q = \\pm q_Z(c)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_X(a)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:453
msgid "`q.to_euler_external_XZY()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"XZY\", external=true)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:459
msgid "It currently returns the intrinsic X-Y-Z angles $(a, b, c)$ with $q = \\pm q_X(a)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_Z(c)$, not an X-Z-Y decomposition (see the warning above)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:463
msgid "`q.to_euler_external_YZX()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"YZX\", external=true)`: angles about Y, Z, X with $q = \\pm q_X(c)\\,q_Z(b)\\,q_Y(a)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:471
msgid "`q.to_euler_external_ZYX()` is `q.to_euler()`: angles about Z, Y, X with $q = \\pm q_X(c)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_Z(a)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:479
msgid "`q.to_euler_internal_XYZ()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"XYZ\", external=false)`: angles about X, Y, Z with $q = \\pm q_X(a)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_Z(c)$. It inverts `from_euler(a, b, c)` with the default order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:487
msgid "`q.to_euler_internal_XZY()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"XZY\", external=false)`: angles about X, Z, Y with $q = \\pm q_X(a)\\,q_Z(b)\\,q_Y(c)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:495
msgid "`q.to_euler_internal_YZX()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"YZX\", external=false)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:501
msgid "It currently returns $(c, b, a)$ with $q = \\pm q_X(a)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_Z(c)$, the reversed intrinsic X-Y-Z angles, not a Y-Z-X decomposition (see the warning above)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:505
msgid "`q.to_euler_internal_ZYX()` is `q.to_euler(order=\"ZYX\", external=false)`: angles about Z, Y, X with $q = \\pm q_Z(a)\\,q_Y(b)\\,q_X(c)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:511
msgid "Every intrinsic variant is the extrinsic variant of the reversed order with the result reversed, because $q_A(a)\\,q_B(b)\\,q_C(c)$ is both intrinsic $ABC$ and extrinsic $CBA$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:513
msgid "Conversion through `Double`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:517
msgid "`DoubleConvert` converts a component type to and from `Double`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:528
msgid "Square roots, trigonometric functions and `@math.pow` are only available on `Double`, so `magnitude`, `normalize`, `from_axis_angle`, `from_euler`, `slerp`, `pow_by_T` and the Euler-angle conversions go through this trait. For `Double` both directions are the identity. For `Int`, `from_double` is `Double::to_int`, which truncates toward zero, so results of these functions over `Int` are truncated."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:530
msgid "The trait is declared `pub`, not `pub(open)`, so code outside this package cannot implement it: `Int` and `Double` are the only component types that work with the functions above. You can still call its methods directly:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:540
msgid "Other methods"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:544
msgid "`q.map(f)` applies `f` to every component, possibly changing the component type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:559
msgid "`q.to_string()` renders `q` as `w + xi + yj + zk` using `Show` of the components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:565
msgid "Negative components keep their sign after the plus, as in `1 + -2i + 3j + 4k`. String interpolation `\"\\{q}\"` and `inspect` use the same text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:569
msgid "`q.equal(r)` (the `==` operator) compares the four components exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:575
msgid "For `Double` components this is IEEE equality: `0.0 == -0.0`, and a quaternion with a NaN component is not equal to itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:579
msgid "`q.hash()` hashes the four components, consistently with `equal`."
msgstr ""

